<article class="env-lemma" id="0G6L">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0G6L">Lemma <span data-tag="0G6L">15.32.1</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $f_1, \ldots , f_ r \in R$ be an Koszul-regular sequence. Then the extended alternating Čech complex $R \to \bigoplus \nolimits _{i_0} R_{f_{i_0}} \to \bigoplus \nolimits _{i_0 < i_1} R_{f_{i_0}f_{i_1}} \to \ldots \to R_{f_1\ldots f_ r}$ from Section <a href="/tag/0G6F" data-tag="0G6F">15.30</a> only has cohomology in degree $r$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemma <a href="/tag/062G" data-tag="062G">15.31.4</a> and induction the sequence $f_1, \ldots , f_{r - 1}, f_ r^ n$ is Koszul regular for all $n \geq 1$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0625" data-tag="0625">15.29.4</a> any permutation of a Koszul regular sequence is a Koszul regular sequence. Hence we see that we may replace any (or all) $f_ i$ by its $n$th power and still have a Koszul regular sequence. Thus $K_\bullet (R, f_1^ n, \ldots , f_ r^ n)$ has nonzero cohomology only in homological degree $0$. This implies what we want by Lemma <a href="/tag/0913" data-tag="0913">15.30.6</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>